+means "repeated in the papers we have"; if a chapter has fewer than 5 past-paper questions the
+bot says it cannot judge. Textbook exercises are never presented as important.
 
 ## Known limitations
 
 * The embedding model is English-only; Roman-Urdu questions rely on the LLM rewrite step.
 * Chunks never cross page boundaries, so a definition split over two pages is split in two.
 * MCQ answers that are not in the sources are solved by the LLM (and labelled as such).
+* Many BM25 misses come from a few 350-word chunks that hold several definitions at once (for
+  example one chunk holds the definitions of acceleration, displacement and velocity); section-aware
+  chunking is the next experiment.
+* Roman-Urdu queries in the gold set are mostly one-keyword questions ("Friction kya hai?"), which
+  are hard for any retriever; check `--translate` before quoting the Hinglish rows.
